<Guide lang="en" slug="preparing-your-spanish-home-for-winter" toc={toc} faq={faq} dienst="sleutelbeheer" ctaTitle="Have your home prepared for winter?" ctaText="We go through the checklist for you, take photos and keep an eye on the house while you are away.">
  <h2 id="waarom">Why prepare for winter?</h2>
  <p>Winter in the Axarquía is mild, but for a house it is the toughest season. After a dry summer most of the rain falls in October and November, sometimes within a few hours. That is when you find out where the roof terrace leaks, whether the gutters are blocked and whether the drainage around the house works. Then comes a cool, damp period in which a closed-up house turns musty and mouldy.</p>
  <p>If you spend the winter in the UK or elsewhere, you only see this when you come back. A few routine checks in the autumn prevent most damage. Below are the checklists we use ourselves.</p>

  <h2 id="herfst">Autumn checklist: before the first rain</h2>
  <p>Do this in September or early October, before the first heavy downpour.</p>
  <ul class="checks">
    <li>Clean gutters, downpipes and the outlets on the roof terrace</li>
    <li>Check the roof terrace for cracks, loose tiles and open joints</li>
    <li>Check a tiled roof for slipped or broken tiles</li>
    <li>Clear drainage channels and gratings on terraces and the drive</li>
    <li>Check drainage along the back wall and behind retaining walls</li>
    <li>Check windows, doors and shutters for sealant and closing</li>
    <li>Have the chimney swept if you have a wood stove</li>
    <li>Have the air con cleaned and tested on heating</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Unsure about the roof or roof terrace? See <a href="/en/problems/roof-leaks/">what to do about a leaking roof</a> and <a href="/en/costs/roof-and-roof-terrace/">what repairs cost</a>.</p>

  <h2 id="vertrek">Checklist when you leave</h2>
  <ul class="checks">
    <li>Main water valve closed; on a finca, pump off unless the garden needs water</li>
    <li>Water heater off</li>
    <li>Fridge emptied and left ajar, or left running with someone checking it</li>
    <li>Dehumidifier on, with a hose to the drain</li>
    <li>Vents open, windows and shutters closed</li>
    <li>Mattresses on edge or covered, cushions and textiles in closed cupboards</li>
    <li>Valuables put away, alarm on</li>
    <li>Pool covered or maintenance arranged</li>
    <li>Garden furniture, parasols and loose items inside or secured</li>
    <li>Keys and contact details with your key holder</li>
  </ul>
  <p>In mountain villages above about 500 metres, such as Cómpeta, Canillas or Alfarnate, it can freeze at night. Drain outdoor pipes and those in unheated rooms there.</p>

  <h2 id="winter">What can go wrong in winter</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Leaks after a storm:</strong> water seeping through a ceiling for weeks. See <a href="/en/problems/roof-leaks/">roof leaks</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>Damp and mould:</strong> a closed, cold house without ventilation. See <a href="/en/problems/damp-and-mould/">damp and mould</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>Power cut:</strong> the freezer defrosts, the alarm goes off-line. See <a href="/en/problems/power-keeps-tripping/">power keeps tripping</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>Flooding:</strong> mud against the back wall or a cracking retaining wall. See <a href="/en/problems/flooding-and-retaining-walls/">flooding and retaining walls</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>Burglary or squatters:</strong> a house that is visibly empty. See <a href="/en/problems/burglary-and-squatters/">burglary and squatters</a>.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="storm">Checklist after a storm</h2>
  <p>After a heavy rain or storm warning from AEMET (orange or red), it is wise to have someone check within a few days.</p>
  <ul class="checks">
    <li>Ceilings and walls under the roof and roof terrace for wet patches</li>
    <li>Gutters and outlets clear, no standing water on the roof terrace</li>
    <li>Retaining walls, slopes and the access track</li>
    <li>Consumer unit: is everything still on?</li>
    <li>Windows, shutters and awnings</li>
    <li>Trees and branches on or against the house</li>
    <li>Photos for the insurer: see the guide <a href="/en/guides/home-insurance-spain/">insurance and damage</a></li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="voorjaar">Spring and summer checklist</h2>
  <ul class="checks">
    <li>Start up the pool, check filter and pump</li>
    <li>Clean the air con before the warm months</li>
    <li>Clear the land: in Andalusia you are required to remove dry vegetation around the house against wildfires</li>
    <li>Switch the irrigation to summer mode</li>
    <li>Rinse the solar panels after dust and Saharan sand</li>
    <li>If you let: test septic tank, water pressure and air con before the first guests</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Month by month, what the weather does and what to do to your house is on <a href="/en/weather/">Axarquía weather and climate</a>.</p>

  <h2 id="toezicht">Who keeps an eye on things?</h2>
  <p>A neighbour who looks in now and then is worth their weight in gold. But after a storm, a leak or a power cut you want someone who can act straight away, send photos and call in a tradesman. That is <a href="/en/key-holding/">key holding</a>: regular checks following these checklists, a report with photos, and before you arrive a house that is aired, clean and ready.</p>
</Guide>
